     There's a known bug in the game restore code (dealing with reloading
object descriptions, naturally) which primarily affects MSDOS, but Atari TOS
is also affected.  AIX on RS/6000 is the only multi-user system that has
ever reported having the problem.  (Draw your own conclusions about this.)

     There's an "unofficial" source code patch for it at linc.cis.upenn.edu
in pub/NH3.0/PC (that's the directory holding the MSDOS binaries; I don't
remember the exact file name, but you won't be able to miss it).  It was
posted to this newsgroup about a year ago.  The text accompanying the patch
claims that it's only for MSDOS, but that is incorrect.  It's needed on
other systems (see above), and can be benignly applied to _any_ of the ports.
(It will have absolutely no effect for Mac and Amiga, because they use
different code.  It has no apparent effect one way or the other for VMS
and almost all varieties of Unix, because the event which triggers the
problem does not occur; AIX+RS/6000 is the only known exception.)

     There are actually two bugs involved, not just one.  Once you tried
to restore your game, you introduced corruption into the data.  There is
no sensible way to recover it now.  (Conceivably you could do some binary
editing on the save file, but I think that it would be hopeless.)  The
binaries available from linc were rebuilt after this "unofficial" patch
was applied; anybody using them is not at risk from this restore bug.

     3.1 handles this issue differently, and this particular bug will be
thoroughly eradicated.  No doubt new & improved bugs will take its place...

